// Always add 2 edges for Node->Node (if A>0). Wait, if A=0, flow is blocked? No, if A=inf, we just pass. If A=0, it's blocked.\n                            // Standard Max Closure doesn't have node splitting. \n                            // Let's assume we MUST add i->i+N. Even if A=0, it blocks flow (which is correct if A is capacity).\n                            // So we add 2 edges (fwd/rev) for i->i+N.\n                            // Total edges: \n                            // if B>0: 2 (S->i) + 2 (i->i+N) = 4 edges (2 logical edges)\n                            // if B<0: 2 (i->T) + 2 (i->i+N) = 4 edges\n                            // if B>0 and B<0? Impossible.\n                            // We need to update `edge_ptr` correctly.\n                            // \n                            // Wait, we need to be careful. `edge_ptr` is the index of the Logical Edge Object.\n                            // One Logical Edge Object uses 2 consecutive indices (fwd/rev).\n                            // So adding one logical edge increments `edge_ptr` by 2.\n                            // \n                            // So, for B>0: Add Logical Edge (S->i). `edge_ptr` += 2.\n                            // For B<0: Add Logical Edge (i->T). `edge_ptr` += 2.\n                            // Always: Add Logical Edge (i->i+N). `edge_ptr` += 2.\n                            \n                            // Let's put this in logic.\n                            \n                            // Temporary registers to hold values for this cycle.\n                            // We are in state LOAD_NODE_PROCESS.\n                            // We use `node_idx`, `A_val`, `B_val`.\n                            \n                            // We need to perform writes.\n                            // Let's define indices for clarity.\n                            // `ep` = current edge_ptr.\n                            // `ep2` = ep + 2.\n                            // `ep4`